  "family": "classical",
  "lineage": "Fourteenth-century French compositional style codified in Philippe de Vitry's 1322 treatise of the same name, introducing isorhythmic motet construction, mensural rhythmic notation, and increased rhythmic-and-harmonic complexity. Guillaume de Machaut (c.1300-1377) is the principal composer; his Messe de Nostre Dame (c.1365) is the first known complete polyphonic setting of the Mass Ordinary by a single composer. Distinguished from ars antiqua by ars nova's sophisticated rhythmic-isorhythmic and notational developments.",
